/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype ExaGRyPE


@image html two-black-holes.png

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents

The ExaGRyPE extension solves the Einstein equations

@f{eqnarray*}{
    R_{ab}-&\frac{1}{2} g_{ab}R+\nabla_{a} Z_{b}+\nabla_{b} Z_{a}-g_{ab}\nabla^c
    Z_c \\
    &-\kappa_{1}[n_{a} Z_{b}+n_{b} Z_{a}+\kappa_{2} g_{ab} n_{c}
    Z^{c}]  =  8\pi T_{ab}
@f}

in various formulations. Noabely, we transfer it into 

@f$
  \partial_t Q +\nabla_i F_i(Q) +B_i (Q) \nabla_i Q = S(Q).
@f$

Collecting all derived quantities and the helper variables $b^i$ from the gauge
condition yields an evolving system over 24 variables

@f$
  Q(t) = 
  \left( \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta,
  \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i \right)(t),
@f$

with a first-order time-derivative and second-order spatial derivatives defined in the 
hypersurface. Let

@f$
   A_{i}:=\partial_{i} \alpha, \ B_{k}^{i}:=\partial_{k} \beta^{i}, \ D_{k i
   j}:=\frac{1}{2} \partial_{k} \tilde{\gamma}_{i j}, \
   P_{i}:=\partial_{i} \phi
@f$


be auxiliary variables. Adding them to the system and treating them as further
evolving variables yields a system of 58 independent variables with 

@f$
  Q(t) = 
  \left(
   \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta, \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i, A_k, B^i_k,
    D_{kij}, P_k \right).
@f$


- \subpage tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_howto
